`assets/moon-phases.svg` is the supplied prototype's schematic illustration of four phase views, checked against NASA's phase descriptions. It is explicitly labeled as a diagram in the website and PDF. Right-lit first quarter and left-lit third quarter represent a Northern Hemisphere view. The dark new Moon is a schematic marker; it is usually not visible in the sky. The PDF redraws the same four shapes as vectors. This is the only original scientific illustration retained; the illustrated Moon cover has been replaced with NASA imagery.

### Reuse

Use follows [NASA Images and Media Usage Guidelines](https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/): NASA content is generally not subject to US copyright and can be used for educational and informational purposes under the stated conditions. Credits are preserved and no NASA endorsement is claimed. Protected identifiers and separately copyrighted third-party material have additional rules. This manifest records source and use information rather than assigning a blanket license to all NASA materials.

## Science verification

| Reader claim | Supporting primary source |
| --- | --- |
| Earth's natural satellite; relative size; rocky surface; thin exosphere; exploration | [NASA Moon Facts](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/) |
| Average distance 384,400 km; orbit about 27.3 days | [NASA Basics of Space Flight, Chapter 1](https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter1-2/) |
| Reflected sunlight; phase cycle about 29.5 days; visible lit fraction changes | [NASA Moon Phases](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/) |
| Rotation matches the orbit; far side also receives sunlight | [NASA Tidal Locking](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tidal-locking/) and [Top Moon Questions](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/top-moon-questions/) |
| Impacts, long-lived craters, mountains, ancient lava plains | [NASA Moon Craters](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/lunar-craters/) and the Tycho record above |
| Ice in permanently shadowed polar regions; no rivers or oceans of liquid water | [NASA Moon Water and Ices](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-water-and-ices/) and Moon Facts |
| Apollo 11 astronauts, photography and sampling | Apollo image record above and [NASA Moon Facts](https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/) |

The 27.3-day orbital period and 29.5-day phase cycle refer to different motions. The Moon must travel a little farther to return to the same phase because Earth and Moon are also moving around the Sun. Regular phases are not Earth's shadow; a lunar eclipse is a separate event. “Nearly the same side” allows for the Moon's small apparent wobble (libration).
